> The method: a linear transition with a nonlinear output

Let the four 64-bit state words be \((a,b,c,d)\). All shifts truncate to
64 bits, all arithmetic wraps modulo \(2^{64}\), and \(\oplus\) means
bitwise XOR. The output before the state advances is

$$
y=9\,\operatorname{rotl}(5b,7)\pmod{2^{64}}.
$$
\alttext(The output is nine times a seven-bit left rotation of five times the second state word, modulo two to the sixty-four.)

Multiplication by an odd integer is invertible modulo \(2^{64}\), and a
rotation is a permutation of bit positions. The composition is therefore
a permutation of 64-bit words: a uniform input word stays uniform. Carries
make multiplication nonlinear over bitwise XOR, obscuring the linear
structure of the underlying engine. This is not a cryptographic claim.

Writing the in-place reference transition as simultaneous assignments gives

$$
T(a,b,c,d)=\left(a\oplus b\oplus d,\ a\oplus b\oplus c,\
a\oplus c\oplus(b\ll17),\ \operatorname{rotl}(b\oplus d,45)\right).
$$
\alttext(The four replacement state words are XOR combinations of the old words, one truncated left shift and one rotation.)

Each operation is linear over \(\mathbf F_2\), so \(T\) is a binary
linear transformation on 256 state bits. The all-zero state is absorbing
and must be excluded. Seed construction fills the four words with
successive SplitMix64 outputs. Its odd counter increment visits distinct
counter words, and its invertible scrambling maps those to distinct
outputs, so these four words cannot all be zero. A direct-state constructor
also rejects the all-zero state.

>> Deriving the inverse, without confusing reversibility with period

Suppose the next state is \((u,v,w,z)\). Put
\(q=\operatorname{rotr}(z,45)=b\oplus d\) and \(t=w\oplus v\).
Then

$$
a=u\oplus q,\qquad t=b\oplus(b\ll17),\qquad
b=t\oplus(t\ll17)\oplus(t\ll34)\oplus(t\ll51),\qquad
c=v\oplus a\oplus b,\qquad d=q\oplus b.
$$
\alttext(The old state can be recovered by undoing the rotation, an XOR-shift inverse using shifts 17, 34 and 51, and the remaining XOR assignments.)

The finite XOR-shift inverse works because four left shifts of 17 bits
erase a 64-bit word: the geometric series in the shift operator terminates.
This proves the transition is reversible. Reversibility alone does not
prove every nonzero state belongs to a single cycle. The authors' primitive
characteristic polynomial supplies the stronger period result
\(2^{256}-1\); a short sample cannot establish that period experimentally.
A 64-bit seed also selects only a small subset of possible initial states.

> Jumping to distant starting points

Let \(\chi(X)\) be the transition's degree-256 characteristic polynomial.
Reduce a desired power of \(X\) modulo \(\chi\). By the
Cayley–Hamilton identity, evaluating that reduced polynomial at \(T\)
gives the same state jump:

$$
p_J(X)=X^J\bmod\chi(X),\qquad
T^J s=p_J(T)s=\bigoplus_{j=0}^{255}[X^j]p_J(X)\,T^j s.
$$
\alttext(Reduce X to the jump distance modulo the characteristic polynomial. The resulting coefficients select an XOR of at most 256 successive transformed states, equal to the distant target state.)

The library uses the published polynomials for `jump()` at distance
\(2^{128}\) and `long_jump()` at \(2^{192}\). Each needs 256 transition
steps and XOR accumulation, rather than that enormous number of explicit
steps. The following table starts from the authors' state order
\((1,2,3,4)\). The regression suite checks these states and output words
against compiled upstream reference C code.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| position | s0 | s1 | s2 | s3 | next output |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (int mode : {0, 1, 2}) {
    dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(std::array<std::uint64_t,4>{1,2,3,4});
    if (mode == 1) rng.jump();
    if (mode == 2) rng.long_jump();
    const auto s = rng.state();
    std::print("| {} | {:016x} | {:016x} | {:016x} | {:016x} | {:016x} |\n",
        mode == 0 ? "initial" : mode == 1 ? "2^128 jump" : "2^192 jump", s[0], s[1], s[2], s[3], rng.next_u64());
  }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| position | s0 | s1 | s2 | s3 | next output |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| initial | 0000000000000001 | 0000000000000002 | 0000000000000003 | 0000000000000004 | 0000000000002d00 |
| 2^128 jump | 8c7a153956b5f3d1 | 701f1a713401d85e | 6527f66a65469085 | 8386b786c4408050 | bbd2f312298443d8 |
| 2^192 jump | 096a8eb71295a400 | dbf84991e50f4516 | 534ee745810d2a0e | 31655ca1a2215bf1 | 527752a1d792704d |
// result_end
\caption(Exact raw states and outputs, rendered as hexadecimal words so no 64-bit integer is rounded through a floating-point column.)

Streams starting \(j2^{128}\) raw transitions apart have disjoint state
ranges provided each consumes less than \(2^{128}\) outputs and the ranges
do not wrap around the full period. This is spacing, not a proof of
statistical independence. A raw-state snapshot excludes a cached second
normal variate in the common distribution interface; jumps clear that
cache so a previous stream's cached draw cannot be returned after a jump.
Copying an entire generator object preserves both its raw state and cache.

> Testing more than one seed

The existing battery checks marginal uniformity, uniform serial pairs,
lag-one correlation, two raw bit frequencies and the raw low-byte counts.
Its p-values are interpreted under an ideal random-stream model. One
unremarkable run is weak evidence, and one small p-value is not automatically
a reproducible defect.

For a continuous, correctly calibrated test statistic, its null p-value
is uniform on \((0,1)\). Running the battery on 40 fresh seeds lets us
inspect each test's p-value distribution with another KS test. Discrete
counts and approximate first-level reference distributions make this
uniformity approximate. With 40 ideal runs, about two p-values below 0.05
and two above 0.95 are expected per test; seven second-level tests also
create multiple opportunities for an apparently unusual result.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xoshiro_bit_frequencies.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar one(2026), bits(2026);
  std::print("{}\n", dm::random::quality_tests(one, 100000).to_md(5));
  const auto repeated = dm::random::quality_tests_over_seeds([](std::uint64_t seed) {
    return std::make_unique<dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar>(seed);
  }, 40, 100000);
  std::print("\nSeeds 1 to 40:\n\n{}\n", repeated.select({"test", "mean_p", "small_p_count", "large_p_count", "ks_p_value"}).to_md(5));
  const auto follow_up = dm::random::quality_tests_over_seeds([](std::uint64_t seed) {
    return std::make_unique<dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar>(seed);
  }, 40, 100000, 41);
  std::print("\nSeeds 41 to 80:\n\n{}\n", follow_up.select({"test", "mean_p", "small_p_count", "large_p_count", "ks_p_value"}).to_md(5));
  auto frequencies = dm::random::bit_frequencies(bits, 100000);
  std::vector<double> positions(64);
  std::iota(positions.begin(), positions.end(), 0);
  for (auto& f : frequencies) f = (f - 0.5) * 2 * std::sqrt(100000.0);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().points(positions, frequencies, "bit-frequency z", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .abline_h(3, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1).abline_h(-3, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1)
      .title("Raw-bit frequencies over 100000 xoshiro256** outputs")
      .x_label("bit position (0=lowest)").y_label("(frequency - 1/2) / ideal SE")
      .y_limits(-4.5, 4.5).size(900, 480).save_svg("build/xoshiro_bit_frequencies.svg");
}
```
