// result_end
\caption(A single battery, two separately reported 40-seed p-value summaries, and raw-bit frequencies from a fresh seed-2026 engine. The horizontal lines in the figure are pointwise three-standard-error references, not simultaneous confidence bounds.)
\alttext(A scatter plot of standardised frequencies for all 64 output bits, with reference lines at plus and minus three standard errors.)

The first range gives an unusually high second-level KS p-value, about
0.998, for the uniformity-KS test. A separately reported follow-up on seeds
41 to 80 instead gives a small serial-pair second-level p-value, about
0.0079. Both ranges are retained. With fourteen second-level results and
an exploratory follow-up, neither isolated extreme should be turned into
a claim of certification or a reproducible generator defect.

These small diagnostics are not substitutes for a large dedicated PRNG
suite or an analysis of high-dimensional dependencies. Adjacent seeds do
not mathematically guarantee independent streams. We use published jump
spacing for the simulation-stream comparison below.

> Applying it: a bootstrap with an exact conditional variance

Let the waiting times be \(x_1,\ldots,x_n\), and draw a resampled
observation uniformly from those \(n\) values. Conditional on the data,
its mean is \(\bar x\) and its variance uses the denominator \(n\).
The mean of \(n\) independent resampled values therefore has

$$
\mathbb E_*[\bar X^*]=\bar x,\qquad
\operatorname{Var}_*(\bar X^*)=\frac1{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_i-\bar x)^2
=\frac{n-1}{n^2}s^2.
$$
\alttext(The conditional bootstrap mean has expectation equal to the sample mean and variance equal to the sum of squared centred observations divided by n squared, or n minus one times the usual sample variance divided by n squared.)

This is an exact oracle for the mean under the resampling model, not an
asymptotic standard-error formula. The bootstrap draws come through
`uniform_u64(0,n-1)`, whose multiply-and-reject mapping is unbiased.
Using a raw word modulo \(n\) would generally be biased when \(n\)
does not divide \(2^{64}\). The new bootstrap overload accepts the
caller-owned engine and reuses the existing interval calculations.
`BootstrapOptions::seed` controls only the default-engine overload; the
supplied engine's seed 2026 determines the draws here.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xoshiro_bootstrap.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto waiting = dm::datasets::faithful().get_column("waiting").values();
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar rng(2026);
  const auto result = dm::stats::Bootstrap({5000, 0.95, 42}).run(waiting, dm::mean, rng);
  const double n = waiting.size();
  const double exact_se = dm::sd(waiting) * std::sqrt(n - 1) / n;
  std::print("| n | mean (min) | exact conditional SE | simulated SE | simulated bias | percentile lower | percentile upper |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n"
             "| {:.0f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",
      n, result.estimate, exact_se, result.standard_error, result.bias,
      result.percentile_interval.first, result.percentile_interval.second);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::hist(result.replicates, 40, "bootstrap means", dm::RGB{147, 197, 253})
      .abline_v(result.estimate, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .title("5000 resampled mean waiting times")
      .x_label("mean waiting time (minutes)").y_label("replicates")
      .size(900, 480).save_svg("build/xoshiro_bootstrap.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | mean (min) | exact conditional SE | simulated SE | simulated bias | percentile lower | percentile upper |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 272 | 70.897059 | 0.822800 | 0.840913 | -0.015371 | 69.264430 | 72.536765 |
\image(figures/xoshiro_bootstrap.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The 5000-replicate bootstrap and exact conditional standard error. The red line marks the original sample mean; interval endpoints are percentile quantiles of the simulated means.)
\alttext(A near-bell-shaped histogram of bootstrap mean waiting times, centred near the original sample mean of about 71 minutes.)

The simulated standard error is 0.84091 minutes, about 2.2% above the exact
conditional value 0.82280. Under the approximate normal-replicate model,
Monte Carlo error for this standard-deviation estimate is about 0.00823,
so the difference is roughly 2.2 Monte Carlo standard errors. Independent
Python arithmetic reproduces all 5000 resampled means exactly, and R
reproduces the summary and percentile endpoints. That verifies the
calculation while leaving the finite-simulation difference visible.

> Jump-separated streams and Monte Carlo variation

The next example copies a stream's starting state for each bootstrap, then
jumps the untouched starting-state generator before making the next copy.
This gives starts exactly \(2^{128}\) raw transitions apart, rather
than adding a jump after a variable amount of resampling work.

Each run uses 2000 replicates. If those mean replicates are approximately
normal, the Monte Carlo standard error of their estimated standard deviation
is approximately \(\sigma_*/\sqrt{2(B-1)}\). The plotted reference band
uses twice that quantity; it is a rough diagnostic for simulation noise,
not a confidence interval for the geyser's population mean.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xoshiro_streams.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto waiting = dm::datasets::faithful().get_column("waiting").values();
  const double n = waiting.size(), exact = dm::sd(waiting) * std::sqrt(n - 1) / n;
  const double band = 2 * exact / std::sqrt(2.0 * (2000 - 1));
  dm::random::Xoshiro256StarStar start(2026);
  std::vector<double> ids, se;
  std::print("| stream | estimated SE | exact conditional SE |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int k = 0; k < 8; ++k) {
    auto stream = start;
    const auto result = dm::stats::Bootstrap({2000, 0.95, 42}).run(waiting, dm::mean, stream);
    ids.push_back(k); se.push_back(result.standard_error);
    std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n", k, result.standard_error, exact);
    start.jump();
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().points(ids, se, "stream estimate", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .line({0, 7}, {exact, exact}, "exact model SE", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .line({0, 7}, {exact - band, exact - band}, "rough MC band", dm::RGB{100, 100, 100}, 1)
      .line({0, 7}, {exact + band, exact + band}, "", dm::RGB{100, 100, 100}, 1)
      .title("Bootstrap SE estimates from eight jump-spaced streams")
      .x_label("stream index").y_label("standard error (minutes)")
      .size(900, 480).save_svg("build/xoshiro_streams.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| stream | estimated SE | exact conditional SE |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0.837298 | 0.822800 |
| 1 | 0.839694 | 0.822800 |
| 2 | 0.843221 | 0.822800 |
| 3 | 0.829831 | 0.822800 |
| 4 | 0.833505 | 0.822800 |
| 5 | 0.840068 | 0.822800 |
| 6 | 0.822840 | 0.822800 |
| 7 | 0.812786 | 0.822800 |
\image(figures/xoshiro_streams.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Estimated bootstrap standard errors across eight jump-spaced starting states, with the exact conditional model value and a rough Monte Carlo variation band.)
\alttext(Eight estimated bootstrap standard errors near the exact conditional standard error, with horizontal reference and Monte Carlo variation-band lines.)

Increasing the number of replicates reduces simulation noise, not the
uncertainty inherent in the observations. This resampling model treats
records as exchangeable. Serial dependence between eruptions would need
a different resampling scheme, such as a block bootstrap; generator
quality cannot repair a misspecified sampling model.

> Conclusion

The transition is a reversible binary linear map; the output scrambler is
a word permutation, and the maximal period relies on the published
primitive polynomial. Reference-code agreement and small statistical
batteries check different properties. On the geyser dataset, a caller-owned
xoshiro stream drives the existing bootstrap API and can be checked against
an exact conditional variance. Jump spacing organises reproducible stream
starting points, while the variation of their estimates keeps Monte Carlo
error separate from the data model's uncertainty.

> References

- D. Blackman and S. Vigna (2021). *Scrambled Linear Pseudorandom Number Generators*.
  ACM Transactions on Mathematical Software 47(4). https://doi.org/10.1145/3460772
- Authors' xoshiro256** 1.0 transition and jump source, https://prng.di.unimi.it/xoshiro256starstar.c
- B. Efron (1979). *Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife*.
  Annals of Statistics 7(1), 1–26. https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176344552
- D. E. Knuth. *The Art of Computer Programming*, volume 2, section 3.3.1.
  https://www-cs-faculty.stanford.edu/~knuth/taocp.html
